Now I can see the full chat_template.jinja. Let me analyze the key section for thinking mode:

```jinja
{%- if add_generation_prompt %}
    {{- '<|im_start|>assistant\n' }}
    {%- if enable_thinking is defined and enable_thinking is false %}
        {{- '<think>\n\n</think>\n\n' }}
    {%- endif %}
{%- endif %}
```

This means:
1. When `add_generation_prompt=True`, it adds `<|im_start|>assistant\n`


2. The empty `<think>` block only appears when `enable_thinking` is explicitly set to `false` — otherwise the model can think freely
3. The template doesn't actually parse `/no_think` from the system message; instead, `enable_thinking` needs to be passed as a template parameter to control thinking behavior

The current code calling `apply_chat_template()` doesn't pass `enable_thinking=False`, so the `/no_think` directive in the system message is just treated as regular text. During SFT training, the TRL trainer likely doesn't pass this parameter either, but since the training data lacks `<think>` tokens in assistant responses, the model learns to output JSON directly. At inference time with greedy decoding, the model should produce JSON as the most likely continuation, though with sampling at higher temperatures the behavior becomes less predictable.

The solution is to pass `enable_thinking=False` to `apply_chat_template()` calls in both the evaluation and GRPO training code, which will force the template to insert an empty `<think></think>` block and prevent the model from generating thinking tokens. Alternatively, I could modify the chat template itself to always disable thinking rather than making it conditional.

Looking back at the previous handoff, it seems the fix was to modify the tokenizer's chat_template directly. But I'm realizing the `/no_think` in the system prompt is just text the model sees—the template doesn't actually parse it—and none of the scripts are passing the `enable_thinking` parameter at all. Let me check if the base Qwen3 model's official template even handles `/no_think`.

